  "captions": [
    {
      "start": 0.0,
      "end": 2.14,
      "text": "Welcome to Divisibility and Remainders."
    },
    {
      "start": 2.68,
      "end": 5.86,
      "text": "Some Australian Mathematics Competition problems look as if"
    },
    {
      "start": 5.86,
      "end": 7.22,
      "text": "they need hundreds of calculations."
    },
    {
      "start": 8.38,
      "end": 9.22,
      "text": "Often they do not."
    },
    {
      "start": 9.8,
      "end": 12.88,
      "text": "The useful idea is to keep only what matters after dividing:"
    },
    {
      "start": 13.5,
      "end": 16.84,
      "text": "the remainder. Let's see it in an easy problem, a medium"
    },
    {
      "start": 16.84,
      "end": 18.46,
      "text": "problem, and a hard one."
    },
    {
      "start": 19.52,
      "end": 23.06,
      "text": "Easy problem. Is two thousand, four hundred and seventy-three"
    },
    {
      "start": 23.06,
      "end": 24.34,
      "text": "divisible by three?"
    },
    {
      "start": 25.0,
      "end": 26.3,
      "text": "You could divide the whole number."
    },
    {
      "start": 26.78,
      "end": 28.1,
      "text": "Instead, look at its digits."
    },
    {
      "start": 28.76,
      "end": 31.16,
      "text": "Why should adding the digits tell us anything about"
    },
    {
      "start": 31.16,
      "end": 31.8,
      "text": "divisibility?"
    },
    {
      "start": 32.86,
      "end": 36.38,
      "text": "Every ten, hundred or thousand leaves remainder one when"
    },
    {
      "start": 36.38,
      "end": 37.46,
      "text": "divided by three."
    },
    {
      "start": 38.08,
      "end": 40.62,
      "text": "So the digit sum has the same remainder as the original"
    },
    {
      "start": 40.62,
      "end": 44.92,
      "text": "number. Two plus four plus seven plus three equals sixteen."
    },
    {
      "start": 45.58,
      "end": 47.14,
      "text": "Sixteen leaves remainder one."
    },
    {
      "start": 47.7,
      "end": 49.5,
      "text": "The number is not divisible by three."
    },
    {
      "start": 50.77,
      "end": 54.71,
      "text": "Medium problem. A display repeats six symbols in this order:"
    },
    {
      "start": 55.15,
      "end": 59.47,
      "text": "triangle, square, circle, star, diamond, hexagon."
    },
    {
      "start": 60.09,
      "end": 62.617,
      "text": "Which symbol is at position one thousand, seven hundred and"
    },
    {
      "start": 62.617,
      "end": 66.33,
      "text": "thirty-nine? Never try drawing thousands of symbols."
    },
    {
      "start": 66.95,
      "end": 69.63,
      "text": "Draw one cycle and give each position a number."
    },
    {
      "start": 70.77,
      "end": 75.67,
      "text": "One thousand, seven hundred and thirty-nine equals six times"
    },
    {
      "start": 75.67,
      "end": 77.57,
      "text": "two hundred and eighty-nine, plus five."
    },
    {
      "start": 78.17,
      "end": 81.05,
      "text": "There are two hundred and eighty-nine complete cycles, with"
    },
    {
      "start": 81.05,
      "end": 82.61,
      "text": "five positions left over."
    },
    {
      "start": 83.13,
      "end": 84.53,
      "text": "The fifth symbol is the diamond."
    },
    {
      "start": 85.21000000000001,
      "end": 88.78999999999999,
      "text": "A remainder of zero would mean the last, or sixth, symbol."
    },
    {
      "start": 90.22,
      "end": 94.4,
      "text": "Hard problem. Find the smallest three-digit whole number that"
    },
    {
      "start": 94.4,
      "end": 97.74000000000001,
      "text": "leaves remainder four when divided by five, leaves remainder"
    },
    {
      "start": 97.74000000000001,
      "end": 100.97999999999999,
      "text": "six when divided by seven, and is divisible by nine."
    },
    {
      "start": 101.64,
      "end": 104.36,
      "text": "Three conditions look difficult until you notice the pattern:"
    },
    {
      "start": 104.97999999999999,
      "end": 107.25999999999999,
      "text": "both remainders are one less than the divisor."
    },
    {
      "start": 108.35,
      "end": 109.65,
      "text": "Call the number N."
    },
    {
      "start": 110.11,
      "end": 113.13,
      "text": "Then N plus one must be a multiple of both five"
    },
    {
      "start": 113.13,
      "end": 117.28999999999999,
      "text": "and seven, so N plus one is thirty-five times a whole"
    },
    {
      "start": 117.28999999999999,
      "end": 121.77,
      "text": "number k. This gives N equals thirty-five k minus one."
    },
    {
      "start": 122.17,
      "end": 125.25,
      "text": "We have replaced two conditions with one useful expression."
    },
    {
      "start": 126.38,
      "end": 128.64,
      "text": "We also need N divisible by nine."
    },
    {
      "start": 129.3,
      "end": 132.56,
      "text": "So thirty-five k must leave remainder one when divided by"
    },
    {
      "start": 132.56,
      "end": 136.3,
      "text": "nine. Thirty-five leaves remainder eight, which is the same as"
    },
    {
      "start": 136.3,
      "end": 139.0,
      "text": "minus one. Thus k leaves remainder eight."
    },
    {
      "start": 139.48,
      "end": 142.915,
      "text": "The smallest positive choice is eight, giving N equals two"
    },
    {
      "start": 142.915,
      "end": 143.68,
      "text": "hundred and seventy-nine."
    },
    {
      "start": 144.64,
      "end": 148.12,
      "text": "Check: the remainders are four and six, and its digits sum"
    },
    {
      "start": 148.12,
      "end": 148.68,
      "text": "to eighteen."
    },
    {
      "start": 150.05,
      "end": 154.35,
      "text": "Notice the three levels: a digit sum, a repeating cycle, and"
    },
    {
      "start": 154.35,
      "end": 156.35,
      "text": "a number satisfying several restrictions."
    },
    {
      "start": 157.31,
      "end": 160.45,
      "text": "The same tool connects them: simplify to a remainder without"
    },
    {
      "start": 160.45,
      "end": 161.95,
      "text": "losing the original conditions."
    },
    {
      "start": 162.77,
      "end": 163.59,
      "text": "Now take your turn."
    },
    {
      "start": 163.95,
      "end": 167.37,
      "text": "Start the lesson problems, explain the key step, and check"
    },
    {
      "start": 167.37,
      "end": 168.11,
      "text": "each result."
    }
  ]
}
